-- What this work costs everywhere else (14 chunks)
insert into public.kb_documents (org_id, kind, url, title, body, source, content_hash, enabled)
select o.id, 'market', 'https://matthew.mccluster.org/sites.html#market', 'What this work costs everywhere else', 'At least a third below what this work costs around here. True of every line, because any line that was not true of was moved until it was.

Ranges published in 2026 pricing guides for small-business work in these markets and nationally. They are NOT quotes from named local companies, they are NOT what any particular competitor charges, and they must never be presented as either.

Checked 2026-08-29 for New Haven, Connecticut and Atlanta, Georgia.
These are the figures as of the date checked. Market rates move; if the checked date is more than six months old, say the number is from a guide rather than from this week.

## The discount, in one line
Nothing here costs more than 67% of the market median for the same work. The ceiling is a CEILING and not a target: a price already below it is left alone rather than raised up to it. Raising the cheap end to hit a discount number exactly would put the price up on the people this discount exists to reach, and would end the free build, which is the hook the whole offer rests on.

## A small-business website, built
Everywhere else: $1,500 to $8,000 one-time.
Here: free — the build is included, not billed.
That line is not charged at all here.
A third off that median is $2,345, which is the most this may cost.
Freelancers $1,500–$8,000. Agencies $5,000–$30,000. A basic site for a local service business in New Haven starts around $1,500–$4,500; Atlanta small-business projects land $2,000–$10,000.

## Hosting and maintenance
Everywhere else: $50 to $500 per month.
Here: $33 a month.
That is below the bottom of the band.
A third off that median is $100.50, which is the most this may cost.
Freelancers $50–$200/mo. Agencies $150–$500/mo. Connecticut packages commonly $50–$325/mo. Yearly, that is $1,000–$6,000 for a small-business site.

## Social media management, on its own
Everywhere else: $500 to $4,000 per month.
Here: $875 a month — and that runs the WEBSITE as well as the feeds.
That sits inside the band rather than under it.
A third off that median is $1,005, which is the most this may cost.
44% of small businesses spend $500–$2,000/mo. Boutique agencies $1,000–$4,000/mo for two or three platforms. Full done-for-you work commonly $2,500/mo. This band is for SOCIAL ALONE, with no website management in it.

## A professional headshot session
Everywhere else: $500 to $1,500 the session.
Here: $285.
That is below the bottom of the band.
A third off that median is $502.50, which is the most this may cost.
One to two hours, three to ten retouched images.

## Event photography
Everywhere else: $150 to $500 per hour.
Here: $201 an hour.
That sits inside the band rather than under it.
A third off that median is $201, which is the most this may cost.
Event photographers charge $150-$500 an hour. This rate was $310 — inside the band rather than under it — until the golden discount was applied; it is now at the ceiling.

## A photography half-day
Everywhere else: $800 to $2,000 up to four or five hours.
Here: $938.
That sits inside the band rather than under it.
A third off that median is $938, which is the most this may cost.
For smaller projects a half-day of four to five hours runs $800-$2,000. This rate was $2,210, ABOVE the band, until the golden discount brought it to the ceiling. It is the one price on this list that had to come down rather than already being under.

## A photography full day
Everywhere else: $1,500 to $4,000 eight hours.
Here: $1,842.50.
That sits inside the band rather than under it.
A third off that median is $1,842.50, which is the most this may cost.
A full day runs $1,500-$4,000 for smaller projects. Experienced commercial and advertising photographers run $3,000-$10,000 for the photographer alone, before studio, models, styling or retouching.

## A produced video day
Everywhere else: $1,500 to $8,000 eight hours, crew and kit.
A third off that median is $2,010, which is the most this may cost.
Compared against the PRODUCED tier, not a bare freelance videographer at $600-$1,200 a day, because these days include the kit, the crew and the edit. A minimal five-person crew starts near $2,400 for one shoot day; a full crew is $3,000-$8,000.

## A produced video half-day
Everywhere else: $900 to $4,800 up to five hours.
A third off that median is $1,206, which is the most this may cost.
Half-days run 60-70% of a full day rather than half, which is why the band is not simply the day band halved.

## A music video
Everywhere else: $2,000 to $30,000 one day, one location.
A third off that median is $3,350, which is the most this may cost.
Shoestring shoots $2,000-$5,000. A professionally produced music video averages $5,000-$30,000. Commercial productions go far past that and are not the comparison here.

## A brand film
Everywhere else: $15,000 to $50,000 shoot and edit.
A third off that median is $13,400, which is the most this may cost.
A produced 60-second brand video runs $15,000-$75,000; brand films and multi-day productions $20,000-$50,000.

## How to use these numbers
- State the band, then state the McCluster price, and stop. Let them do the subtraction.
- Never say ''cheaper than everyone'' or ''the lowest price''. Some of these prices are not below the band, and a claim that is wrong once is a claim nobody believes again.
- Never name a competitor and never attribute a price to one.
- A band is a band. Do not quote the midpoint as if it were a fact about a specific business.
', 'ledger', '5433:bb65d449', true
  from public.orgs o where o.slug = 'mccluster'
on conflict (org_id, kind, coalesce(url, title)) do update
  set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body,
      content_hash = excluded.content_hash, enabled = true, updated_at = now();

